  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166813,
      "id": "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b",
      "title": "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "dddfee84-2a8c-5f44-ad4d-bb0b8dbd5363",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$\n- **II.** $({y}^{′})^{2}+x{y}^{′}={y}^{5}$\n- **III.** ${y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\n- Equation III is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "fb341180-ae0e-5ecf-9e53-b4c122177daa",
