      "title": "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d177d916-f09d-56ab-af12-193419b326b8",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\sqrt{{y}^{″}+x}=3{y}^{4}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$\n- **III.** $\\sqrt{{y}^{‴}+x}=3{y}^{4}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of third-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "d46f4f2a-5e43-5eea-b99a-bd4e0984d5b1",
