      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are second-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${y}^{′}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$\n- **II.** ${y}^{′}+3x{y}^{′}={x}^{5}y$\n- **III.** $({y}^{″})^{2}+x{y}^{′}={y}^{5}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of second-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′},$ so this is a first-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a second-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
